<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><em>In which the Guardian staff pick one g=
ame each day that we think is worth watching. Broadcast info is US and all =
times are ET. Games for Thursday to Sunday will be covered in our midweek e=
dition of the newsletter.</em></p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>Monday:</strong> The mid-to-lower =
part of the Premier League table is crowded, with just three points separat=
ing seventh from 16th place. Everton and Newcastle United are in the middle=
 of that mass, and will look to separate themselves when they meet at St Ja=
mes=E2=80=99 Park. <em><strong style=3D"color: #0077B6; font-style: normal;=
 font-weight: 700;">Newcastle v Everton</strong></em> is at 3pm on USA.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>Tuesday</strong>: The Champions Le=
ague throws up no shortage of intriguing matchups this week, but Aston Vill=
a visiting Barcelona should be the most fun on Tuesday. The Villans have be=
en perfect in Europe so far this season, but will travel to face one of the=
 continent=E2=80=99s premier attacking sides. <em><strong style=3D"color: #=
0077B6; font-style: normal; font-weight: 700;">Barcelona v Aston Villa </st=
rong></em>is at 3pm on Paramount+ and TUDN.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>Wednesday</strong>: Along with Vil=
la, Real Betis have been one of the surprise packages of the Champions Leag=
ue so far, also with three wins in three. Like Villa on Tuesday, they=E2=80=
=99ll face a good test in one of the best atmospheres in Europe: Dortmund=
=E2=80=99s yellow wall. <em><strong style=3D"color: #0077B6; font-style: no=
rmal; font-weight: 700;">Borussia Dortmund v Real Betis</strong></em> is at=
 3pm on Paramount+ and TUDN.</p>
